course: dictionary
lesson: 4943
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning. Context, the words and sentences around the word, tells you which meaning the writer wants. The word impression has four main meanings: a feeling or idea you get about someone, a mark pressed into a soft surface, a funny copy of a famous person, and a strong effect on people.
explain: «People have laughed at leaders for hundreds of years, with cartoons, impressions and fake news shows called satire.» Impressions sits next to cartoons and fake news shows, which are both kinds of comedy. So impressions here means funny copies of how people talk and act. Ask: what would fit here? Then put your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
explain: «An impression copies how a famous person talks and moves.» This sentence gives you the clue itself: copies. When you see a word with more than one meaning, read the whole sentence before you choose. The first meaning you think of is not always the right one.
fact: An impression is a feeling or idea you get about someone or something, especially at first.
fact: A footprint in mud is an impression, a mark pressed into a soft surface.
fact: An impression can be a funny copy of how a famous person talks or moves, and pupils may do impressions of characters in drama.
fact: To make an impression is to have a strong effect on people.
fact: Useful phrases: first impression, make a good impression, have the impression that, do an impression of.
exercise: «People have laughed at leaders for hundreds of years, with cartoons, impressions and fake news shows called satire.» What does impressions mean here? | Feelings you get about someone | Strong effects on people | *Funny copies of how people talk and act | Marks pressed into mud
exercise: Sam says, «My first impression of the new teacher was that she was kind.» What does impression mean here? | A funny copy of the teacher's voice | A strong effect on the whole school | A mark pressed into soft clay | *A feeling or idea you get about someone, especially at first
exercise: The fossil is an impression of a leaf pressed into rock. What is an impression here? | A funny copy of a famous person | To have a strong effect on people | A feeling you get about someone | *A mark pressed into a soft surface
test: Mia does an impression of her teacher in the school play. What is an impression here? | A strong effect on people | A mark pressed into mud | *A funny copy of how someone talks and moves | A feeling about someone, especially at first
test: Which phrase means to have a strong effect on people? | Do an impression of | First impression | Have the impression that | *Make an impression
exam: «He wanted to make a good impression on the judges.» What does make an impression mean here? | *Have a strong effect on people | Copy how the judges talk | Feel sad about the judges | Leave a mark in wet cement
